#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# ball

#% text
# `ball([x, y, z], r)` is a solid ball in `graphics3d`, drawn as its surface.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `ball()` is `ball([0, 0, 0])`, and `ball(c)` the ball of radius 1 about `c`. `ball(n)` for an integer `n` is the unit ball of `n` dimensions.

#% item
# Coordinates are in the units of the graphic; the box around the scene follows the data unless `plot_range` says otherwise.

#% item
# Colours, `opacity` and `edge_form` before it in its list style it; faces are lit.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The default:

#% cell
ball()
#% output
#= ball([0, 0, 0])

#% cell
graphics3d(ball())
#% output
#= image("ball.files/ball-2.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Balls of several radii:

#% cell
graphics3d(table(ball([k, 0, 0], k/4), k, 4))
#% output
#= image("ball.files/ball-3.svgz")

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A ball and a sphere look alike:

#% cell
graphics_row([graphics3d(ball()), graphics3d(sphere())])
#% output
#= image("ball.files/ball-4.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `graphics3d`, `sphere`, `plot3d`
